<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Bind typed parameters, use omop_study and shortcuts, then derive and filter columns without bringing rows to your computer.
</div>

This chapter uses the public synthetic Synthea27Nj numeric connection from [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
Run its sections in order. Each chapter starts from a fresh practice connection;
all the outputs below come from real dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.4 calls.
The numbers describe teaching data, not clinical findings.

## Describe the setting as a parameter

A *parameter* is a named, typed placeholder in a study definition. It lets you
reuse a recipe without editing its logic. It is not R code or SQL text.

```{r parameter}
setting <- omop_param("setting", type = "concept_set")
class(setting)
```

This parameter accepts concept sets. A separate date parameter would declare a
date type; give each changing input its own appropriate type.

## Build a template

```{r template}
template <- omop_study(omop_event("visit_occurrence", concepts = setting),
  outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
    prior_visits = omop_count("visit_occurrence", window = omop_window(-365, -1)),
    age = omop_age()))
class(template)
```

`omop_study()` returns an ordinary recipe. It creates an indexed population and
baseline output. We explicitly exclude day zero from the prior-visit count.
The unbound template cannot execute until its required setting is supplied.

## Bind and inspect

```{r bind}
outpatient <- recipe_bind(template, setting = 9202L)
stopifnot(recipe_explain(outpatient, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
recipe_explain(outpatient)$selected
```

Binding fills the placeholder while preserving the template. Concept 9202 is the
outpatient visit setting, not the record's provenance type.

```{r run}
print(recipe_execute(outpatient, out = c(baseline = "D_param_outpatient"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_param_outpatient$age", datasources = conns)
```

The server computes annual age (index year minus birth year) at the first outpatient visit. The analyst
receives only the approved summary.

## Read the simple-layer defaults

```{r defaults}
print(outpatient)
```

The study's default index selection is `first`. The population uses the default
coverage policy; all requirements are combined with AND. Baseline has episode
rows. For survival, supply outcomes and an explicit follow-up definition. For
example, `follow_up = list(start = 1, end = 365, censor = c("observation_end",
"death"))` describes a year after entry, ending sooner at a listed censor.
The [output chapter](book-08-outputs.html) executes the corresponding native survival format.

## Shortcuts expand into ordinary variables

Use a shortcut when its defaults match your question; use an explicit reducer
when you need more control. `before = 365` includes day zero, while `after = 365`
starts at day one. Without either, most shortcuts use all history through entry.

```{r shortcut}
shortcuts <- omop_study(omop_event("measurement", 3000963L), outputs = "baseline",
  baseline = list(ever = omop_ever("measurement", 3000963L, before = 365),
    count = omop_count("measurement", 3000963L, before = 365),
    value = omop_value_at_index("measurement", 3000963L,
      tolerance = c(-30, 0), unit = 8713L)))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(shortcuts, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(shortcuts, out = c(baseline = "D_shortcuts"), conns = conns))
```
```{r shortcut-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_shortcuts$ever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
```

Everyone has ever = 1 because the index hemoglobin measurement itself is included. To ask about
measurements strictly before entry use `window = omop_window(-365, -1)` instead.
First/last/mean/min/max-value shortcuts use the same event and window model.
`omop_history()` bundles named ever variables; it does not define a new language.

## Mutate a protected table

*Mutate* adds or replaces a column inside the server object. It accepts a closed
set of arithmetic and conditional expressions, not arbitrary R functions.

```{r mutate}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_shortcuts", twice = count * 2L,
  high = ifelse(value >= 14, 1L, 0L), newobj = "D_derived", conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_derived", datasources = conns)
```

Bare names refer to columns. Unknown values stay unknown in the comparison.
`newobj` makes the output explicit; omitting the input would use the most recent
output and make a tutorial harder to follow.

```{r mutate-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_derived$high", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
```

The high flag remains private; this table counts its values. Division by zero and
nonfinite arithmetic results become missing. `case_when`, `coalesce` and `cut`
are supported for declared recoding and bands; arbitrary function evaluation is refused.

## Filter using a predicate

A *predicate* is a condition. Only TRUE rows survive a filter; missing conditions
are dropped. Filtering creates another private object with its own release check.

```{r filter}
print(ds.omop.filter("D_derived", expr = omop_where(high == 1L),
  newobj = "D_high", conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_high$value", datasources = conns)
```

The cutoff of 14 g/dL is an arbitrary teaching value, not a clinical threshold.
This subset is large enough in Synthea27Nj. A predicate cannot read
protected person/episode keys to target a known person. These verbs work on
assigned `omop.table` objects, not on arbitrary database tables.

## Recap

Parameters change declared inputs, shortcuts express common native recipes, and mutate/filter operate privately on assigned tables. Each final object still passes publication checks.

## Try it

Bind the same template to inpatient visits and summarise the prior-visit count. Do not alter the outpatient template in place.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r solution}
inpatient <- recipe_bind(template, setting = 9201L)
stopifnot(recipe_explain(inpatient, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(inpatient, out = c(baseline = "D_param_inpatient"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_param_inpatient$prior_visits", datasources = conns)
```
Each binding is a separate recipe. Its own index changes which earlier records fall into the lookback window. A numeric summary suits these varied counts; a categorical table can be refused when it creates too many distinct levels in a small population.

</details>
